# ThreadSanitizer suppressions — Nous Engine
#
# Used via:  TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<repo>/Tools/Sanitizers/tsan.supp
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING AN ENTRY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file starts empty ON PURPOSE. Every suppression is a report you will
# never see again, so add one only after reading the stack and concluding the
# race is genuinely not ours. Pre-emptively suppressing "probably Mesa" noise
# is how a real bug goes unnoticed for a year.
#
# The reason false positives happen at all: vcpkg dependencies are built
# WITHOUT -fsanitize=thread. TSan cannot see the synchronization performed
# inside an uninstrumented library, so it reports happens-before edges it
# cannot prove. Expect candidates from Mesa/llvmpipe (which spawns a worker
# thread per core), SDL3, FFmpeg and miniaudio's realtime device thread.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SYNTAX
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   race:<function or file substring>       suppress a data race
#   race_top:<function>                     match only the top frame
#   thread:<function>                       suppress a thread-leak report
#   mutex:<function>                        suppress a mutex misuse report
#   deadlock:<function>                     suppress a lock-order inversion
#   called_from_lib:<soname>                suppress everything originating in
#                                           a given uninstrumented library
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXAMPLES (commented out — enable only if the stacks actually justify it)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# called_from_lib:libvulkan_lvp.so
# called_from_lib:libLLVM.so
# called_from_lib:libSDL3.so
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACTIVE SUPPRESSIONS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-08-22 after triaging the first TSan run of EditorApp on the Fedora VM.
# All three were verified by reading the stacks: the racing frames are entirely
# inside third-party code, and the Nous frames present are only the *creation*
# sites of the thread or mutex involved (VulkanBackend.cpp:91 / :139 are where
# vkCreateInstance/vkCreateDevice happen to spawn Mesa's internals).
#
# Matching is by substring against function names, deliberately NOT
# `called_from_lib:`. A whole-library suppression would also hide a genuine race
# of ours that merely happened to be reported through a Mesa or layer frame.

# --- Mesa llvmpipe: its fence objects race their own condvar/mutex on teardown.
# Reported as two data races between the 'llvmpipe-N' worker threads, in
# lp_fence_create / lp_fence_destroy / cnd_broadcast. Mesa is built without
# -fsanitize=thread, so TSan cannot see the synchronization it does internally.
race:lp_fence_
mutex:lp_fence_

# --- Vulkan validation layer (VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation): lock-order inversion
# between its own rwlocks, in the object_lifetimes tracker. Present only while
# validation layers are enabled; not reachable from engine code.
deadlock:object_lifetimes::
